    {
      "id": "b80337cbd9d986d046570d19a41d01dc8b311de5",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1426348237,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I have a sample file in https://github.com/Lekensteyn/wireshark-notes/blob/master/tls/pgsql-ssl.pcapng that contains an Error message:\n\n```\ncapinfos -k pgsql-ssl.pcapng | grep -o 'CLIENT_R.*' > keys.txt\neditcap --inject-secrets tls,keys.txt pgsql-ssl.pcapng pgsql-ssl-dsb.pcapng\nwireshark -r pgssl-ssl.pcapng -Y 'pgsql.type == \"Error\"'\n```\n\nI found a spec at https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol-message-formats.html\n\n> ErrorResponse (B)\n>\n> * `Byte1('E')` - Identifies the message as an error.\n> * `Int32` - Length of message contents in bytes, including self.\n> * The message body consists of one or more identified fields, followed by a\n>   zero byte as a terminator. Fields can appear in any order. For each field\n>  there is the following:\n>   * `Byte1` - A code identifying the field type; if zero, this is the message\n>    terminator and no string follows. The presently defined field types are\n>    listed in Section 55.8. Since more field types might be added in future,\n>    frontends should silently ignore fields of unrecognized type.\n>   * `String` - The field value.\n\nBased on https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol-message-types.html, `String` is a zero-terminated string.\n\nThe message definition can be found in https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/protocol-overview.html#PROTOCOL-MESSAGE-CONCEPTS\n\nAnalysing the current code:\n```c\n    case 'E':\n    case 'N':\n        length -= 4; // remove length of the length field itself\n        while ((signed)length > 0) {\n            c = tvb_get_guint8(tvb, n);\n            if (c == '\\0')\n                break;\n            // after this, 'siz' will contain the length excluding NUL terminator\n            s = tvb_get_stringz_enc(pinfo->pool, tvb, n+1, &siz, ENC_ASCII);\n            // <snip>\n            length -= siz+1; // remove length for the field value including NUL\n            n += siz+1;\n        }\n```\n\nSo indeed, the `length` variable is too large at the end as the `Byte1` field type is not removed. However, as the documentation points out, the last field should be `followed by a zero byte as terminator`. So normally the `c == '\\0'` check ensures proper loop termination.\n\nThis patch is technically an improvement, but it should normally not make a difference.\n\n@abasargin How did you discover this accounting issue, where is it exactly causing problems for you?",
          "author": {
            "id": 2415432,
            "username": "Lekensteyn",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Peter Wu",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/efcf30db53f30b15e7c905ef37bd4dc44d87573bd92baf97cd707115d3fcd0c0?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/Lekensteyn"
